# Wave 35 §S follow-up — the "nothing to do" auto-pass regression lane E found
# in play.spec.ts ("a spell resolves unattended" failed 5/5 after F's R15 land
# fix): `decideStop` only auto-passed a priority window when Forge's
# actionable set was EMPTY, and R15 now puts every untapped tap-only-mana land
# in that set at ordinary priority, so a seat with any untapped land left is
# never idle by that old measure.
#
# Seat 0 (human) casts a 1-mana creature, leaving TWO Mountains untapped; seat
# 1 (ai — an ordinary human-controlled seat, never Forge's AI, per this file's
# own README) has one untapped Mountain and an empty hand, so once the spell
# is on the stack seat 1 has nothing real to do at all — only land.
#
# Three Mountains for seat 0 is deliberate, not incidental: the scenario
# harness's own pre-existing actionable-cache race (`PlayBridge.java`'s
# `refreshActionable` doc — the initial `selectCard` after a scenario applies
# occasionally never registers) is MEASURABLY WORSE with only one Mountain on
# the battlefield than with three — the opposite of the obvious guess that
# less state races less. Measured directly, interleaved against
# `two-mana-pass.state` (3 Mountains, otherwise identical shape) under
# identical host load: one Mountain failed roughly half of all runs at the
# cast itself; three Mountains, 16/18 across two batches. Not explained
# further here — `auto-pass-land.jvm.test.ts`'s own retry (clicking the hand
# card again while it is still unselected, never a second time once it is
# not) absorbs most of what is left.
turn=1
activeplayer=human
activephase=MAIN1
